n fmt.Errorf(\"instance %q forest contains NUL\", instance.ID)\n\t}\n\tif instance.Forest == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"instance %q forest is empty\", instance.ID)\n\t}\n\tif instance.Host != \"\" {\n\t\tif err := validateSSHHost(instance.Host); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"instance %q host: %w\", instance.ID, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := validateSSHPath(instance.Root, \"root\"); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"instance %q: %w\", instance.ID, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := validateSSHExecutable(instance.Forest); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"instance %q: %w\", instance.ID, err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// validateRouteIdentifier is deliberately narrower than a shell token. These\n// values become URL/query route identities as well as Forest operands, so path\n// separators, controls, and shell punctuation are never accepted.\nfunc validateRouteIdentifier(value, what string) error {\n\tif value == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"%s is empty\", what)\n\t}\n\tif value == \".\" || value == \"..\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"%s %q is not an identifier\", what, value)\n\t}\n\tfor _, r := range value {\n\t\tif unicode.IsSpace(r) || unicode.IsControl(r) {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"%s %q contains whitespace or control characters\", what, value)\n\t\t}\n\t\tswitch r {\n\t\tcase '/', '\\\\', '?', '#', '&', ';', '|', '`', '$', '\\'', '\"', '<', '>', '*', '(', ')', '[', ']', '{', '}', '!', '=':\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"%s %q contains unsafe character %q\", what, value, r)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nfunc validateSSHHost(host string) error {\n\tif host == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"host is empty\")\n\t}\n\tif strings.HasPrefix(host, \"-\") {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"host %q may not begin with '-'\", host)\n\t}\n\tif containsUnsafeShellCharacters(host) || strings.IndexFunc(host, unicode.IsSpace) >= 0 {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"host %q contains unsafe characters\", host)\n\t}\n\t// Validate the optional user@ prefix without trying to resolve DNS here.\n\tif strings.Count(host, \"@\") > 1 {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"host %q has more than one user separator\", host)\n\t}\n\n[99 more lines in file. Use offset=180 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788552325325},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the code. Let me think about the correctness of the change.\n\nThe key behavior changes:\n1. `DiscoverLocalInstances` now calls `resolveDiscoveryCollisions(instances, nil)` to deduplicate IDs and roots among discovered instances.\n2. `syncDiscoveredInstances` computes explicit entries and resolves collisions, renaming discovered IDs and omitting discovered roots that match explicit roots.\n\nNow let me think about potential issues.\n\n**Issue 1: Label validation.** `validateInstance` requires non-empty Label. In `resolveDiscoveryCollisions`, when renaming, `inst.Label = formatLabel(candidate)`. Since candidate is non-empty (base \"instance\" fallback), formatLabel produces non-empty label. OK.\n\nBut wait — what if `inst.ID` is empty and the base becomes \"instance\"? Then candidate \"instance\". If used contains \"instance\" already... disambiguate to \"instance-2\". Then label = formatLabel(\"instance-2\") = \"Instance 2\". OK. And the ID is valid route identifier.\n\n**Issue 2: `resolveDiscoveryCollisions` root dedup with empty root.** Instances passed to it from `DiscoverLocalInstances` are already validated (validateInstance requires non-empty root). But `syncDiscoveredInstances` receives discovered from `DiscoverLocalInstances`, which already validated. However, the test `TestSyncDiscoveredInstancesResolvesCollidingIDs` passes instances with Roots set. OK.\n\nBut `resolveDiscoveryCollisions` is also called with `explicit` list in sync. The explicit list comes from inventory instances, which are validated. OK.\n\n**Issue 3: Ordering/determinism.** The sort uses Root, then ID, then Forest. For the collision resolution, the lexicographically smallest root keeps base ID. But note the test uses sibling roots `agent-test` vs `agent_test` where \"-\" (0x2D) sorts before \"_\" (0x5F). So `agent-test` root gets base ID \"agent-test\", and `agent_test` gets \"agent-test-2\". Deterministic.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle determinism concern. The comment says \"Ordering is by normalized root\". But it sorts by `ordered[i].Root` (raw root), not `filepath.Clean`. For the dedup, it uses `filepath.Clean(inst.Root)` for `seenRoots` and `explicitRoots`. But sort uses raw `Root`. Could two paths that are cleaned to the same value sort differently? E.g., `/tmp//agent` vs `/tmp/agent`. `filepath.Clean` would treat them as same root, dedup would drop one. But sort by raw root would order them differently. However, since they dedup to same root, only the first (in raw-sort order) survives. The surviving ID binding could depend on raw path ordering, which might not be fully deterministic across scan orderings? Actually within a single scan, the input order is from map iteration (nondeterministic in Go). But the sort sorts deterministically by raw Root. So the surviving one among two raw-different-but-clean-equal roots depends on raw sort order, which is deterministic given the same raw strings. OK.\n\nActually, the deeper determinism claim in the test: \"Reordered scans must keep the same route-to-root binding.\" Since sort is by Root (then ID, then Forest), reordering input doesn't change output. Good.\n\n**Issue 4: The critical interaction — discovered instance renaming across scans and the `discovered` map / worker state.**\n\nConsider: On first scan, discovered instances `A` (root1) and `B` (root2) both have ID \"agent-test\". resolve → A keeps \"agent-test\" (root1 sorts first), B becomes \"agent-test-2\". sync adds both: `a.discovered[\"agent-test\"]` and `a.discovered[\"agent-test-2\"]`, states for both, workers for both.\n\nOn second scan, same two checkouts. resolve → same mapping. sync:\n- For existing inventory entries, iterate. Explicit entries (none). Both \"agent-test\" and \"agent-test-2\" are in `a.discovered`, so they're \"discovered\". For each, check `discMap[existing.ID]`. \"agent-test\" → present (root1's instance). \"agent-test-2\" → present (root2's instance). Both updated. Good.\n\nNow what if the explicit inventory also has an entry that collides? The explicit list is built from inventory entries NOT in `a.discovered`. Good.\n\nBut consider a scenario: explicit inventory entry with ID \"agent-test\" and root \"/explicit/agent\". Discovered has root \"/tmp/agent-test\" with ID \"agent-test\" and root \"/tmp/agent_test\" with ID \"agent-test\" (colliding). resolve with explicit:\n- explicitIDs = {\"agent-test\"}, explicitRoots = {\"/explicit/agent\"}.\n- ordered: sorted by root. \"/tmp/agent-test\" (dash) before \"/tmp/agent_test\" (underscore).\n- For \"/tmp/agent-test\": not in explicitRoots. base \"agent-test\", candidate \"agent-test\" is taken (explicitID). disambiguate → \"agent-test-2\" (valid, unused). So ID becomes \"agent-test-2\".\n- For \"/tmp/agent_test\": not in explicitRoots. base \"agent-test\", taken? used now contains \"agent-test\" (explicit) and \"agent-test-2\". So disambiguate → \"agent-test-3\". ID becomes \"agent-test-3\".\n\nHmm, but this is a subtle issue: the discovered checkout \"/tmp/agent-test\" (the one whose natural ID is \"agent-test\") gets \"agent-test-2\", while \"/tmp/agent_test\" gets \"agent-test-3\". That's deterministic. Fine.\n\nBut wait, consider the test `TestSyncDiscoveredInstancesPreservesExplicitOnIDCollision`:\n- inventory explicit: ID \"agent-test\", Root \"/explicit/agent\".\n- discovered: ID \"agent-test\", Root \"/tmp/agent-test\".\n- resolve → explicitIDs={\"agent-test\"}, explicitRoots={\"/explicit/agent\"}. ordered has one item \"/tmp/agent-test\". base \"agent-test\" taken → \"agent-test-2\". Good. Test expects got[0] explicit, got[1] renamed with root /tmp/agent-test. Good.\n\n**Issue 5: `resolveDiscoveryCollisions` omitting discovered roots matching explicit roots — but what about the reverse (discovered root not cleaned vs explicit root cleaned)?**\n\nexplicitRoots uses `filepath.Clean(inst.Root)`. discovered cleanRoot uses `filepath.Clean`. Consistent.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: `explicitRoots` includes ALL explicit roots, even from explicit entries that might have been removed? No, explicit entries are never removed. OK.\n\n**Issue 6: A discovered instance whose root collides with an explicit root but with a DIFFERENT ID.** It's omitted (the explicit route is authoritative). The test `TestSyncDiscoveredInstancesOmitsExplicitRootDuplicate` covers this. Good.\n\n**Issue 7: Potential issue with `DiscoverLocalInstances` now dropping previously-valid discovered instances because of root dedup.** Actually `DiscoverLocalInstances` already used a map keyed by normalized root path (`candidates`). Wait, the map key is `workDir` or `repoPath`, not normalized. The comment says \"keyed by normalized root path\" but actually it's raw. Hmm. In scanSystemdUnits, `out[workDir]`; in scanDevelopmentRoot, `out[repoPath]`. So the map dedups by raw root. Two checkouts with roots differing only by `//` would both appear. Then resolveDiscoveryCollisions dedups by clean root, dropping the second.\n\nActually wait — before this change, `DiscoverLocalInstances` returned `instances` from the map (deduped by raw root), then sorted. Now it additionally resolves collisions (which includes root dedup by cleaned root). This could drop an instance that has a distinct raw root but same clean root. That's arguably correct (same checkout).\n\nBut there's a subtle regression concern: `resolveDiscoveryCollisions` sorts and returns a new slice. The `seenRoots` dedup drops duplicates. But the map `candidates` already dedups by raw root. For systemd units and dev roots, could there be the same checkout discovered by both systemd AND dev scan? Yes! If a dev checkout is also running as a systemd unit, `scanSystemdUnits` would add `out[workDir]` and `scanDevelopmentRoots` would try `out[repoPath]`. If workDir == repoPath (same string), the map dedups. But if workDir is e.g. `/home/user/Development/misty-step/canopy` and repoPath is `/home/user/Development/misty-step/canopy` (same), dedup works. If systemd workDir has trailing slash or symlink difference, they'd be different raw keys but same clean root → resolveDiscoveryCollisions drops the duplicate. But which one is dropped? The one with the larger raw Root (sorted later) is dropped. Hmm, that could matter if the two have different Forest binary paths.\n\nActually this is a pre-existing concern somewhat. Before the change, both raw-different entries would appear as two instances with the same ID \"canopy\" (collision!) — actually before, there was no ID collision resolution, so they'd both have ID \"canopy\", and sync would treat them... hmm. Let me check the pre-change sync behavior. Before, `discMap` keyed by ID, so two discovered with same ID → last wins in map. Then in \"add newly discovered\", only unique IDs. So actually before this change, colliding IDs would collapse to one instance (last in iteration order, nondeterministic). The new change fixes that by renaming.\n\nOK, so the change is a genuine improvement and addresses ID collision.\n\n**Issue 8: `disambiguateID` infinite loop possibility?** It loops from n=2 upward. `used` map grows with each resolved instance. Since validateRouteIdentifier accepts `base-N` for any N (digits and hyphen are valid), eventually an unused candidate exists. The only way infinite loop is if validateRouteIdentifier never passes. But candidate = base + \"-\" + n, where base is a valid ID (or \"instance\"). Since base is valid and \"-\" and digits are valid, candidate is valid. So it always passes validateRouteIdentifier. Actually wait, base could be empty → set to \"instance\" before. So base non-empty. base is valid? The discovered instances come from validateInstance in DiscoverLocalInstances path, so base is valid. But in sync, discovered also from DiscoverLocalInstances (valid). But `resolveDiscoveryCollisions` is a public-ish function also called in tests with arbitrary input. Not a concern for production.\n\nActually, one edge: base could be \".\" or \"..\"? validateInstance would reject those. So no.\n\n**Issue 9: `used` map contains explicit IDs AND resolved discovered IDs. But when renaming, does it correctly mark the base as used if base is the natural ID but it collided with... wait.**\n\nLook at the loop:\n```go\nbase := inst.ID\nif base == \"\" { base = \"instance\" }\ncandidate := base\nif _, taken := used[candidate]; taken {\n    candidate = disambiguateID(base, used)\n}\nif candidate != inst.ID {\n    inst.ID = candidate\n    inst.Label = formatLabel(candidate)\n}\nused[candidate] = struct{}{}\nseenRoots[cleanRoot] = struct{}{}\n```\n\nIf candidate == base == inst.ID and not taken, we mark used[inst.ID]. Good. Next colliding instance with same base: used[base] is now taken → disambiguate → \"base-2\". Mark used. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: `disambiguateID` checks `used[candidate]` and `validateRouteIdentifier`. It doesn't check `seenRoots` — but seenRoots is about roots, not IDs. Fine.\n\n**Issue 10: The `explicit` list in syncDiscoveredInstances.** \n\n```go\nexplicit := make([]Instance, 0, len(a.inventory.Instances))\nfor _, existing := range a.inventory.Instances {\n    if _, wasDiscovered := a.discovered[existing.ID]; !wasDiscovered {\n        explicit = append(explicit, existing)\n    }\n}\ndiscovered = resolveDiscoveryCollisions(discovered, explicit)\n```\n\nThis builds explicit from inventory instances not in `a.discovered`. But there's a subtle issue: the `a.discovered` map is keyed by ID. After the previous change introduced renaming, discovered IDs are stable. But what about an explicit entry that was previously discovered but is now... no, explicit entries are never added to discovered. Only auto-discovered entries get added to `a.discovered`. So `explicit` correctly captures explicit entries.\n\nBut wait, consider the scenario in `TestSyncDiscoveredInstancesPreservesExplicitOnIDCollision` — that's a single sync call on a fresh app. Fine.\n\n**Issue 11: There's a subtle problem with `explicitRoots` using all explicit roots to omit discovered. What if an explicit entry and a discovered entry legitimately share a root but the explicit entry was... no, that's the intended behavior.**\n\nLet me look at the bigger picture. Is there any regression where discovered instances that were previously added (in `a.discovered`) might now get omitted because their root matches an explicit root? No, because if a root was previously added as discovered, there's no explicit entry with that root (explicit entries are authoritative and would have caused the discovered to be omitted from the start).\n\nActually, consider: explicit inventory has root `/explicit/agent` ID \"agent-test\". Discovered scan finds `/tmp/agent-test` (renamed to \"agent-test-2\"). This gets added to `a.discovered[\"agent-test-2\"]`. On next sync, `explicit` = inventory entries not in discovered = the \"agent-test\" explicit entry. resolve(discovered, explicit): discovered root \"/tmp/agent-test\" not in explicitRoots → renamed to \"agent-test-2\". Consistent.\n\n**Issue 12: What about the `selected` and worker state when a discovered instance is renamed?** Since renaming is deterministic and stable, no churn.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: consider a scenario where explicit inventory is EMPTY, and two colliding checkouts exist. First sync: A → \"agent-test\" (root dash), B → \"agent-test-2\" (root underscore). Both marked discovered. selected defaults to inventory.Instances[0].ID = \"agent-test\". Good.\n\nNow if the user later adds an explicit inventory entry \"agent-test\" (via config reload? Actually inventory is loaded once at startup via NewApp, not reloaded). So no dynamic explicit changes. OK.\n\n**Issue 13: `DiscoverLocalInstances` also now calls `resolveDiscoveryCollisions(instances, nil)` which sorts by Root. But then immediately after, the function sorts by ID and returns. The sort by Root inside resolve is only used internally. Fine.\n\nNow, let me think about whether there's a real bug worth flagging. The main issue is that this revision is STALE (diverged from master). That alone requires `changes`. \n\nBut let me also verify there isn't a correctness bug in the change that would be worth mentioning in the summary. Let me think harder.\n\nActually, wait. There might be a subtle issue with the determinism claim and the `explicit` ordering. In `resolveDiscoveryCollisions`, the sort is `sort.Slice` which is NOT stable. The comparison function:\n```go\nri, rj := ordered[i].Root, ordered[j].Root\nif ri != rj { return ri < rj }\nif ordered[i].ID != ordered[j].ID { return ordered[i].ID < ordered[j].ID }\nreturn ordered[i].Forest < ordered[j].Forest\n```\nThis is a total order over (Root, ID, Forest) with no ties left. So even though sort.Slice is unstable, since all three fields together are unique (or if ties exist, unstable sort could reorder equal elements). Could two discovered instances have identical Root, ID, AND Forest? If so, they're truly identical and order doesn't matter. So determinism holds.\n\nActually wait, could two instances have same Root and ID but different Forest? The comparator handles Forest. Could have same Root, same ID, same Forest — identical, order irrelevant. Fine.\n\nNow, the deeper concern: **Is the \"smallest discriminating counter\" claim accurate?** Yes.\n\nLet me think about `formatLabel` producing a label that could be empty. If candidate = \"instance-2\", formatLabel → \"Instance 2\". Non-empty. Good.\n\n**Now a potentially real issue:** In `resolveDiscoveryCollisions`, when `candidate != inst.ID`, it updates `inst.ID` and `inst.Label`. But it does NOT re-run `validateInstance`. The resulting ID \"base-N\" should be valid. But what if base contains characters invalid per validateRouteIdentifier? In production, discovered instances pass validateInstance (ID valid). The base from discovered is valid. Explicit IDs are valid (inventory validated). So OK.\n\nBut actually there's a subtle thing: `sanitizeID` can produce IDs ending in digits, e.g. \"agent-2\" is itself a valid ID. Then a collision on \"agent-2\"... disambiguate → \"agent-2-2\". Fine, valid.\n\nNow, what about the \"base\" being a valid route identifier but `disambiguateID` producing a candidate that's already used as a root? No, IDs vs roots separate.\n\nLet me think about whether the change has any security issue. The IDs become route identities (URL path segments). `validateRouteIdentifier` ensures safety. The renaming uses base + \"-\" + n, all safe. No injection.\n\nNow let me consider: is there a concurrency issue in syncDiscoveredInstances? It holds `a.mu.Lock()` for the whole function. The new `resolveDiscoveryCollisions` is called while holding the lock, but it's a pure function. Fine.\n\nNow, the most important thing: **This revision is stale.** master is at 73d14896 (the \"freshness-cycle-budget\" change), while this branch is based on 674894f9 (route-param-validation). The branch's merge-base with master is 674894f9, and master has moved one commit ahead (73d1489). So this revision cannot fast-forward master.\n\nActually wait, let me double check. The branch `forest/canopy-critic-discovery-id-collision/discovery-id-collision` tip is 34b04b0. Its parent is 674894f9. Master is 73d1489, whose parent... let me check. 73d1489's parent — is it 674894f9 or something else? Let me verify the relationship.\n\nFrom earlier: merge-base of master and HEAD is 674894f9. master..HEAD = just 34b04b0. HEAD..master = just 73d1489. So they diverge at 674894f9. master = 674894f9 + 73d1489. HEAD = 674894f9 + 34b04b0.\n\nSo this revision is stale relative to master. Cannot fast-forward. → `changes`.\n\nLet me confirm 73d1489's parent is 674894f9.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Now I understand the code. Let me think about the correctness of the change.\\n\\nThe key behavior changes:\\n1. `DiscoverLocalInstances` now calls `resolveDiscoveryCollisions(instances, nil)` to deduplicate IDs and roots among discovered instances.\\n2. `syncDiscoveredInstances` computes explicit entries and resolves collisions, renaming discovered IDs and omitting discovered roots that match explicit roots.\\n\\nNow let me think about potential issues.\\n\\n**Issue 1: Label validation.** `validateInstance` requires non-empty Label. In `resolveDiscoveryCollisions`, when renaming, `inst.Label = formatLabel(candidate)`. Since candidate is non-empty (base \\\"instance\\\" fallback), formatLabel produces non-empty label. OK.\\n\\nBut wait — what if `inst.ID` is empty and the base becomes \\\"instance\\\"? Then candidate \\\"instance\\\". If used contains \\\"instance\\\" already... disambiguate to \\\"instance-2\\\". Then label = formatLabel(\\\"instance-2\\\") = \\\"Instance 2\\\". OK. And the ID is valid route identifier.\\n\\n**Issue 2: `resolveDiscoveryCollisions` root dedup with empty root.** Instances passed to it from `DiscoverLocalInstances` are already validated (validateInstance requires non-empty root). But `syncDiscoveredInstances` receives discovered from `DiscoverLocalInstances`, which already validated. However, the test `TestSyncDiscoveredInstancesResolvesCollidingIDs` passes instances with Roots set. OK.\\n\\nBut `resolveDiscoveryCollisions` is also called with `explicit` list in sync. The explicit list comes from inventory instances, which are validated. OK.\\n\\n**Issue 3: Ordering/determinism.** The sort uses Root, then ID, then Forest. For the collision resolution, the lexicographically smallest root keeps base ID. But note the test uses sibling roots `agent-test` vs `agent_test` where \\\"-\\\" (0x2D) sorts before \\\"_\\\" (0x5F). So `agent-test` root gets base ID \\\"agent-test\\\", and `agent_test` gets \\\"agent-test-2\\\". Deterministic.\\n\\nBut wait — there's a subtle determinism concern. The comment says \\\"Ordering is by normalized root\\\". But it sorts by `ordered[i].Root` (raw root), not `filepath.Clean`. For the dedup, it uses `filepath.Clean(inst.Root)` for `seenRoots` and `explicitRoots`. But sort uses raw `Root`. Could two paths that are cleaned to the same value sort differently? E.g., `/tmp//agent` vs `/tmp/agent`. `filepath.Clean` would treat them as same root, dedup would drop one. But sort by raw root would order them differently. However, since they dedup to same root, only the first (in raw-sort order) survives. The surviving ID binding could depend on raw path ordering, which might not be fully deterministic across scan orderings? Actually within a single scan, the input order is from map iteration (nondeterministic in Go). But the sort sorts deterministically by raw Root. So the surviving one among two raw-different-but-clean-equal roots depends on raw sort order, which is deterministic given the same raw strings. OK.\\n\\nActually, the deeper determinism claim in the test: \\\"Reordered scans must keep the same route-to-root binding.\\\" Since sort is by Root (then ID, then Forest), reordering input doesn't change output. Good.\\n\\n**Issue 4: The critical interaction — discovered instance renaming across scans and the `discovered` map / worker state.**\\n\\nConsider: On first scan, discovered instances `A` (root1) and `B` (root2) both have ID \\\"agent-test\\\". resolve → A keeps \\\"agent-test\\\" (root1 sorts first), B becomes \\\"agent-test-2\\\". sync adds both: `a.discovered[\\\"agent-test\\\"]` and `a.discovered[\\\"agent-test-2\\\"]`, states for both, workers for both.\\n\\nOn second scan, same two checkouts. resolve → same mapping. sync:\\n- For existing inventory entries, iterate. Explicit entries (none). Both \\\"agent-test\\\" and \\\"agent-test-2\\\" are in `a.discovered`, so they're \\\"discovered\\\". F