non-empty. base is valid? The discovered instances come from validateInstance in DiscoverLocalInstances path, so base is valid. But in sync, discovered also from DiscoverLocalInstances (valid). But `resolveDiscoveryCollisions` is a public-ish function also called in tests with arbitrary input. Not a concern for production.\\n\\nActually, one edge: base could be \\\".\\\" or \\\"..\\\"? validateInstance would reject those. So no.\\n\\n**Issue 9: `used` map contains explicit IDs AND resolved discovered IDs. But when renaming, does it correctly mark the base as used if base is the natural ID but it collided with... wait.**\\n\\nLook at the loop:\\n```go\\nbase := inst.ID\\nif base == \\\"\\\" { base = \\\"instance\\\" }\\ncandidate := base\\nif _, taken := used[candidate]; taken {\\n    candidate = disambiguateID(base, used)\\n}\\nif candidate != inst.ID {\\n    inst.ID = candidate\\n    inst.Label = formatLabel(candidate)\\n}\\nused[candidate] = struct{}{}\\nseenRoots[cleanRoot] = struct{}{}\\n```\\n\\nIf candidate == base == inst.ID and not taken, we mark used[inst.ID]. Good. Next colliding instance with same base: used[base] is now taken → disambiguate → \\\"base-2\\\". Mark used. Good.\\n\\nBut there's a subtle issue: `disambiguateID` checks `used[candidate]` and `validateRouteIdentifier`. It doesn't check `seenRoots` — but seenRoots is about roots, not IDs. Fine.\\n\\n**Issue 10: The `explicit` list in syncDiscoveredInstances.** \\n\\n```go\\nexplicit := make([]Instance, 0, len(a.inventory.Instances))\\nfor _, existing := range a.inventory.Instances {\\n    if _, wasDiscovered := a.discovered[existing.ID]; !wasDiscovered {\\n        explicit = append(explicit, existing)\\n    }\\n}\\ndiscovered = resolveDiscoveryCollisions(discovered, explicit)\\n```\\n\\nThis builds explicit from inventory instances not in `a.discovered`. But there's a subtle issue: the `a.discovered` map is keyed by ID. After the previous change introduced renaming, discovered IDs are stable. But what about an explicit entry that was previously discovered but is now... no, explicit entries are never added to discovered. Only auto-discovered entries get added to `a.discovered`. So `explicit` correctly captures explicit entries.\\n\\nBut wait, consider the scenario in `TestSyncDiscoveredInstancesPreservesExplicitOnIDCollision` — that's a single sync call on a fresh app. Fine.\\n\\n**Issue 11: There's a subtle problem with `explicitRoots` using all explicit roots to omit discovered. What if an explicit entry and a discovered entry legitimately share a root but the explicit entry was... no, that's the intended behavior.**\\n\\nLet me look at the bigger picture. Is there any regression where discovered instances that were previously added (in `a.discovered`) might now get omitted because their root matches an explicit root? No, because if a root was previously added as discovered, there's no explicit entry with that root (explicit entries are authoritative and would have caused the discovered to be omitted from the start).\\n\\nActually, consider: explicit inventory has root `/explicit/agent` ID \\\"agent-test\\\". Discovered scan finds `/tmp/agent-test` (renamed to \\\"agent-test-2\\\"). This gets added to `a.discovered[\\\"agent-test-2\\\"]`. On next sync, `explicit` = inventory entries not in discovered = the \\\"agent-test\\\" explicit entry. resolve(discovered, explicit): discovered root \\\"/tmp/agent-test\\\" not in explicitRoots → renamed to \\\"agent-test-2\\\". Consistent.\\n\\n**Issue 12: What about the `selected` and worker state when a discovered instance is renamed?** Since renaming is deterministic and stable, no churn.\\n\\nBut there's a subtle issue: consider a scenario where explicit inventory is EMPTY, and two colliding checkouts exist. First sync: A → \\\"agent-test\\\" (root dash), B → \\\"agent-test-2\\\" (root underscore). Both marked discovered. selected defaults to inventory.Instances[0].ID = \\\"agent-test\\\". Good.\\n\\nNow if the us