rrors, state changes, cleanup, and external boundaries.\n4. Check for data loss, unsafe input, secret exposure, races, broken cancellation, partial updates, compatibility breaks, and changed operator workflows.\n5. Exercise the smallest scenario that can disprove each important claim.\n6. Research every suspected finding to its end. Do not report an open hypothesis when the repository can answer it.\n7. Report only problems introduced or exposed by this Revision.\n\nFor each finding, give the location, failure mode, impact, evidence, and required remedy. Set severity from demonstrated risk. Do not inflate it. Do not bury a blocking defect under minor comments.\n\nApprove only when all required Checks pass and no blocking finding remains.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788552399952},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_pscwj2k0wqen1rcxmuby2n3t","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review\ndescription: Perform a strict but calibrated maintainability review of one exact Revision. Use with thermo-nuclear-review during every Iron Forest Verifier review.\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  adapted-from: https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/main/cursor-team-kit/skills/thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review\n---\n\n# Deep code-quality review\n\nSearch for a simpler design that removes concepts, branches, layers, or special cases. Prefer direct and boring code.\n\nCheck whether the Revision:\n\n- Uses the existing architecture and canonical helpers.\n- Puts behavior in the layer that owns it.\n- Adds scattered conditions, modes, flags, or silent fallbacks.\n- Adds wrappers or abstractions that do not reduce complexity.\n- Hides an invariant behind casts, optional values, or loose data shapes.\n- Duplicates logic or creates a second convention.\n- Mixes orchestration with business logic.\n- Makes related state changes less atomic.\n- Grows a file or function until its purpose is hard to scan.\n- Leaves obsolete code, comments, aliases, or temporary scaffolding.\n\nPropose the structural move when you report a problem. Prefer deletion and reframing over moving the same complexity.\n\nBlock approval only for a material maintainability regression or a concrete engineering risk. Mark a cleaner alternative as optional when the current design is clear, consistent, and safe. Do not block on taste, formatting, or a different personal implementation.\n\nReport a few high-confidence findings. Do not produce a list of cosmetic comments.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788552399952},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've read the review skills. Now let me do a focused review of the actual change for correctness, and also verify the stale condition is the blocking issue.\n\nLet me trace the changed paths and think about whether there's a genuine correctness bug worth adding to the summary.\n\nLet me look at the key concern: the `resolveDiscoveryCollisions` with `explicit` in `syncDiscoveredInstances`. Actually, let me think about a subtle bug with the `discovered` map keyed by ID and the deterministic renaming when explicit inventory is also present.\n\nActually, there is a potentially significant subtle bug regarding the interaction between the deterministic renaming and the pre-existing `discovered` map when the set of explicit vs discovered changes. But inventory doesn't reload dynamically, so explicit set is fixed. So no churn.\n\nLet me think about one more scenario that could be a real correctness issue: **Two discovered instances with the same ID but where one is also in `a.discovered` from a previous scan, and a third explicit entry appears.** Not applicable since explicit is fixed.\n\nLet me think about whether `resolveDiscoveryCollisions` could rename an already-discovered instance differently after a rescan due to a NEW discovered instance appearing that sorts before it.\n\nExample: Initially only checkout `agent_test` exists (root `/a/agent_test`, ID \"agent-test\"). resolve → base \"agent-test\", used empty → ID \"agent-test\". sync marks discovered[\"agent-test\"].\n\nLater, a new checkout `agent-test` appears at root `/a/agent-test` (dash), which sorts BEFORE `/a/agent_test` (underscore) since \"-\" < \"_\". Now resolve with two:\n- sorted: `/a/agent-test` (dash) first, then `/a/agent_test`.\n- dash: base \"agent-test\", not taken → keeps \"agent-test\".\n- underscore: base \"agent-test\", taken → \"agent-test-2\".\n\nSo the underscore checkout's ID CHANGES from \"agent-test\" (previous scan) to \"agent-test-2\" (now). This is a binding change: the route-to-root binding for `agent_test` changes when a new sibling appears.\n\nIs this a problem? The comment claims \"repeated scans and input reorderings keep the same route-to-root binding\". But adding a NEW colliding checkout that sorts earlier causes existing checkouts to be renamed. This means:\n- The `agent_test` checkout's ID changes from \"agent-test\" to \"agent-test-2\".\n- Its previous state (in `a.states[\"agent-test\"]`) and worker would be... let me trace sync.\n\nOn the second sync (with both checkouts):\n- discovered resolved = [dash→\"agent-test\", underscore→\"agent-test-2\"].\n- `a.discovered` currently = {\"agent-test\"} (from first scan where underscore was \"agent-test\").\n- `a.inventory.Instances` = [underscore instance with ID \"agent-test\"] (the discovered one from first scan).\n\nReconcile step 1: for existing inventory entry \"agent-test\" (which IS in a.discovered): check discMap[\"agent-test\"]. discMap has dash→\"agent-test\". So stillPresent=true, newInstances appends `dash` instance (root /a/agent-test). So the inventory entry ID \"agent-test\" now points to the DASH root, not the underscore root!\n\nStep 2: add newly discovered. existingMap = {\"agent-test\": true}. For disc \"agent-test\" (dash) → already in existingMap, skip. For disc \"agent-test-2\" (underscore) → not in map, add. So underscore gets added as new \"agent-test-2\", marked discovered, new state/worker.\n\nResult: The underscore checkout loses its old state (was at \"agent-test\", now \"agent-test-2\" with fresh empty state) and its worker restarts. The dash checkout inherits the \"agent-test\" ID and... wait, the existing state at \"agent-test\" was the underscore's snapshot. Now `a.states[\"agent-test\"]` still holds the underscore's old snapshot! Because step 1 just updates `newInstances`, it doesn't touch `a.states`. So the \"agent-test\" state (which contains underscore's last snapshot with Root=/a/agent_test) is now associated with the dash instance (Root=/a/agent-test).\n\nThen a refresh for dash instance \"agent-test\" runs collector with dash root and records snapshot with dash root into state \"agent-test\". So it self-corrects on next refresh. But between the sync and the refresh, `app.state(\"agent-test\")` would show stale underscore snapshot attributed to dash. Actually, this is a transient inconsistency.\n\nIs this a real bug introduced by the change? It's about ID reassignment when new siblings appear. The comment claims determinism for \"repeated scans and input reorderings\", which is true for a FIXED set. But it's not stable under adding/removing colliding checkouts — IDs of existing checkouts can shift, causing stale state attribution and worker churn.\n\nHowever, is this \"introduced or exposed by this Revision\"? Before the change, colliding IDs would collapse into one instance entirely (last-wins, nondeterministic). The new behavior is strictly better. The transient stale state attribution is an edge case. But is it blocking? Probably not blocking for a merge, but it's a real observation.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Is this even reachable? The discovery happens every 5 seconds. When a new checkout appears that collides and sorts earlier, the existing checkout's ID shifts. The old state is left orphaned (not cleaned up!) — wait, let me check.\n\nIn step 1, for existing inventory entry \"agent-test\" (was d